From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roger Luethi <luethi@osism.tech>
Date: Wed, 24 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] nova-cell: identify the hypervisor by address

Make the compute_id restore work on deployments where it currently never
fires, and repair hosts whose identity was overwritten by the removed
fallback.

Where a compute's name canonicalises to an FQDN the lookup misses on
every host, so no compute_id is delivered and a reprovisioned compute is
never restored -- the purpose the task was added for. The miss happens
because the lookup compares ansible_facts.nodename, which is
platform.node() and so the raw short hostname, against
hypervisor_hostname, which for the libvirt driver comes from
virConnectGetHostname() and is canonicalised through
getaddrinfo(AI_CANONNAME).

Names are a poor key here in general. Several distinct machines can
present the same name to any one name-based comparison, so a lookup that
finds exactly one record has still not established that the record
belongs to this host. Matching on the service host (nova's CONF.host)
instead moves the problem rather than solving it: CONF.host can be set
explicitly, and a host that sets it can then be matched to a different
host's record.

Match on the address instead. A hypervisor reports host_ip, which nova
takes from CONF.my_ip and kolla renders from api_interface_address --
the value this role already has for the host it is configuring. Two
computes cannot share a management address in a working deployment,
which is the uniqueness names never had.

The name is still used, but only to corroborate: a record at this
address whose hypervisor_hostname is neither nodename nor nodename as
its first label is rejected. That covers an address inherited from a
decommissioned host whose record still exists -- unless that record's
name happens to corroborate too, which corroboration cannot detect.
Corroboration can only reject, never select, so a wrong rejection costs
a missed restore and never a wrong identity.

Ambiguity is judged on the address alone, before the name is consulted.
Corroborating first would let two records at this address be reduced to
one, hiding the ambiguity from the assertion that exists to catch it.

Where identification fails -- the address overridden, the host renamed,
the record absent -- nothing is delivered and nova keeps whatever
identity it holds. Delivering nothing is only a safe outcome once the
lookup no longer falls back to `hypervisor_nodename | to_uuid` when it
matches nothing; this change depends on that fallback having been
removed.

The scope is a deployment assumption, not a heuristic: that CONF.my_ip
is the address kolla renders from api_interface_address, and that a
management address is not reassigned to a different machine whose name
also corroborates. Neither is something more name matching would fix.

Reading host_ip needs the detail listing, so the query asks for it; the
summary listing returns host_ip unset.
---

Not part of the upstream commit message: this patch is an upstreaming
candidate, to be proposed to kolla-ansible master and the stable branches
and dropped here once it merges. It applies on top of
nova-cell-do-not-invent-a-compute-id.patch and requires it.

--- a/ansible/roles/nova-cell/tasks/config.yml
+++ b/ansible/roles/nova-cell/tasks/config.yml
@@ -67,28 +67,44 @@
           endpoint_type: "{{ openstack_interface }}"
           service: "compute"
           type: "hypervisor"
+          parameters:
+            details: true
       register: hypervisors
       check_mode: false
       become: true
       delegate_to: "{{ groups[nova_cell_conductor_group][0] }}"
 
-    - name: Match current hypervisor
+    - name: Find the hypervisor at this host's address
       ansible.builtin.set_fact:
-        matched_hypervisors: >-
-          {{ hypervisors.resources | selectattr('name', 'equalto', hypervisor_nodename) }}
+        hypervisors_at_address: >-
+          {{ hypervisors.resources | selectattr('host_ip', 'equalto', api_interface_address) | list }}
       when: hypervisors.resources | length > 0
 
+    # Ambiguity is judged on the address alone, before the name is consulted.
+    # Corroborating first could reduce two records at this address to one and
+    # hide the ambiguity from this assertion.
     - name: Assert that only one hypervisor was matched
       ansible.builtin.assert:
         that:
-          - matched_hypervisors | length == 1
+          - hypervisors_at_address | length == 1
         fail_msg: |
-          "Found multiple compute services with same host name, please check your Nova "
-          "compute agents list and fix it. The query with hypervisor name "
-          "'{{ hypervisor_nodename }}' returned following: {{ matched_hypervisors }}"
+          "More than one hypervisor reports the address of this host "
+          "('{{ api_interface_address }}'), so its compute_id cannot be determined "
+          "and none is delivered. Check the Nova compute agents list for stale "
+          "records. Matched: {{ hypervisors_at_address }}"
       when:
         - hypervisors.resources | length > 0
-        - matched_hypervisors | length > 0
+        - hypervisors_at_address | length > 0
+
+    # The name can only reject, never select: a record at this host's address
+    # whose hypervisor_hostname is unrelated to it is not this host's record.
+    - name: Corroborate the hypervisor by name
+      ansible.builtin.set_fact:
+        matched_hypervisors: >-
+          {{ hypervisors_at_address
+             | selectattr('name', 'match', (hypervisor_nodename | regex_escape) ~ '(\.|$)')
+             | list }}
+      when: hypervisors.resources | length > 0
 
     - name: Copy the matched hypervisor UUID to compute_id
       ansible.builtin.copy:
